@node Installation
@chapter Installation
@cindex installation

This chapter walks you through installing everything Codebud needs on
Arch Linux: system packages, Ollama, the Python virtual environment, and
the Codebud package itself.  If you are on another distribution, the
steps are the same in spirit --- substitute your package manager where
@command{pacman} appears.

@menu
* System Requirements::         What hardware and OS you need.
* System Packages::             Installing Python, Node.js, and build tools.
* Installing Ollama::           Getting the local LLM server.
* Cloning Codebud::             Getting the source code.
* Python Environment::          Creating the virtual environment and installing deps.
* Build System::                Building and installing with GNU Autotools.
* Verifying the Install::       Running a quick sanity check.
@end menu


@node System Requirements
@section System Requirements
@cindex system requirements

@table @strong
@item OS
Arch Linux (rolling).  Other systemd-based distributions work with minor
adjustments.
@item RAM
8 GB minimum (for the 3B model).  16 GB recommended.
@item Disk
~3 GB for the recommended model, plus ~100 MB for the code and venv.
@item CPU
Any modern x86-64.  No GPU required; see @ref{Choosing a Model} for
speed expectations.
@item Network
Required once to install packages and pull the model.  The agent itself
runs fully offline after that.
@end table


@node System Packages
@section System Packages
@cindex pacman
@cindex system packages

First make sure your system is up to date:

@example
sudo pacman -Syu
@end example

Then install the required packages in one command:

@example
sudo pacman -S python python-pip python-virtualenv \
               git curl base-devel nodejs npm
@end example

@table @strong
@item python, python-pip, python-virtualenv
Codebud and all its dependencies are pure Python.
@item git
Required to clone the repository.
@item curl, base-devel
Used when building native extensions (some pip packages compile C code).
@item nodejs, npm
Required if you want to install or run the OpenClaw gateway CLI
(@command{openclaw}).  Not needed to run Codebud on its own.
@end table

Verify the key tools are present:

@example
python --version    # expect Python 3.10 or later
git --version
node --version
@end example


@node Installing Ollama
@section Installing Ollama
@cindex Ollama, installation
@cindex AUR

Ollama is the local LLM server.  Codebud sends every prompt to it
over a local HTTP connection.

@subsection Option A --- AUR (recommended on Arch)

If you have an AUR helper installed, use it so that Ollama is managed
by pacman and upgrades cleanly:

@example
yay -S ollama
# or:
paru -S ollama
@end example

@subsection Option B --- Official installer script

If you do not use the AUR, the official script works on any distribution:

@example
curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh
@end example

This installs @file{/usr/local/bin/ollama} and optionally a systemd unit.

@strong{Note:} the AUR package is preferred on Arch because it integrates
with @command{pacman} for upgrades, handles the service unit correctly,
and does not require running an untrusted shell script as root.

@subsection Starting Ollama

After installation, start the server:

@example
# One-off (for testing):
ollama serve

# Permanent (recommended):
sudo systemctl enable --now ollama
@end example

Verify it is running:

@example
curl http://localhost:11434/api/tags
@end example

You should see a JSON response.  If you get @samp{Connection refused},
the server is not running --- start it with one of the methods above.

For full model management instructions (pulling, storing, transferring
models) see @ref{Model Management}.


@node Cloning Codebud
@section Cloning Codebud
@cindex cloning, repository

@example
git clone https://github.com/mkhellat/codebud.git
cd codebud
@end example

The repository layout:

@example
src/codebud/    The package (agent, CLI, tools, default policy)
docs/           This manual (GNU Texinfo)
scripts/        Developer scripts (benchmark, search and plan evals)
tests/          Pytest test suite
bootstrap       Generates ./configure (GNU Autotools)
configure.ac    Autoconf input
Makefile.am     Automake input (also src/ and docs/)
pyproject.toml  Package metadata (PEP 621)
@end example


@node Python Environment
@section Python Environment
@cindex virtual environment
@cindex pip install

Instead of @command{make install} (@pxref{Build System}), Codebud can be
installed with @command{pip} like any Python package.  For development,
create and activate a virtual environment:

@example
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
@end example

Your shell prompt will show @samp{(.venv)} when the environment is
active.  Install Codebud in editable mode together with the development
tools (pip 25.1 or later):

@example
pip install -e . --group dev
@end example

This installs:
@itemize
@item @code{prompt_toolkit}, @code{requests} --- runtime dependencies
(@code{prompt_toolkit} draws the chat command menu and status toolbar),
and on Python 3.10 @code{tomli}, which reads the configuration file
(@pxref{Configuration File}; later Pythons have it built in)
@item @code{pytest}, @code{pytest-cov}, @code{ruff}, @code{build},
@code{numpy} and @code{pyrage} --- the @code{dev} dependency group (PEP
735), for working on Codebud itself (@code{numpy} and @code{pyrage} so the
project-search and encryption tests run)
@item the @command{codebud} CLI entry point (maps to @code{codebud.cli.main:main}); @code{python -m codebud} works too
@end itemize

The optional OpenAI backend (@env{CODEBUD_BACKEND=openai}) needs the
@code{openai} client, which
is not installed by default:

@example
pip install -e ".[openai]"        @r{# from a checkout}
pip install "codebud[openai]"     @r{# from a package index}
@end example

Project search (@pxref{The index Subcommand}) needs @code{numpy}, also
optional:

@example
pip install -e ".[rag]"           @r{# from a checkout}
pip install "codebud[rag]"        @r{# from a package index}
@end example

@noindent
Distribution packages work as well (@code{python3-numpy} on Debian and
Fedora, @code{python-numpy} on Arch, @code{py311-numpy} on FreeBSD).

Encryption at rest with age needs @code{pyrage}, also optional (the
@command{age} command-line tool is not used: it reads passphrases only
from a terminal):

@example
pip install -e ".[crypto]"        @r{# from a checkout}
pip install "codebud[crypto]"     @r{# from a package index}
@end example

All dependencies are declared in @file{pyproject.toml}; there is no
@file{requirements.txt}.

To deactivate the environment later:

@example
deactivate
@end example

To reactivate in a new shell:

@example
source .venv/bin/activate
@end example


@node Build System
@section Build System
@cindex Autotools
@cindex configure
@cindex build system
@cindex GNU make

Codebud builds and installs with GNU Autotools.  The generated
@command{configure} script is portable POSIX @command{sh}; the targets are
GNU/Linux (Debian/Ubuntu, Fedora/RHEL, Arch), macOS and FreeBSD.  So far the
build has been verified on Arch Linux only; reports from the other systems
are welcome.

@subsection Installing from a checkout

A git checkout needs GNU Autoconf (2.69 or later) and Automake (1.16 or
later) to generate @command{configure}, and GNU Texinfo to build the
manual; release tarballs already contain @command{configure} and the info
manual, so skip @command{./bootstrap} there.

@example
./bootstrap                  @r{# git checkout only: generates ./configure}
./configure                  @r{# checks Python, its modules and Ollama}
make                         @r{# builds the launcher and the info manual}
make check                   @r{# runs the test suite (needs pytest)}
sudo make install            @r{# into /usr/local by default}
@end example

@command{configure} needs Python 3.10 or later with the @code{requests}
and @code{prompt_toolkit} modules (and, on 3.10 only, @code{tomli}), and
prints the package names for your system if they are missing.  It warns
if @code{requests} is older than 2.32.4, which fixes credential leaks
(CVE-2023-32681, CVE-2024-35195, CVE-2024-47081); a distribution package
with those fixes backported is fine.  It only warns if @command{ollama} is not
found, since Ollama may run on another machine, if @code{numpy} is
missing, since only project search needs it, if @code{pyrage} is
missing, since only encryption at rest needs it, and if @command{bwrap}
(bubblewrap) is missing, since without it command steps are not isolated
(@pxref{Isolated Steps}).  The summary at the end
shows what was found:

@example
  codebud 0.1.0
    prefix:          /usr/local
    Python:          /usr/bin/python3 (3.14)
    package dir:     $@{PYTHON_PREFIX@}/lib/python3.14/site-packages
    tests (pytest):  yes
    project search:  yes (numpy)
    encryption:      yes (pyrage)
    ollama:          /usr/bin/ollama
    isolation:       /usr/bin/bwrap
@end example

@command{make install} installs:

@itemize
@item the @file{codebud} package (byte-compiled) with its default policy
and skill under Python's @file{site-packages} for the prefix;
@item a small @file{@var{bindir}/codebud} launcher that runs it with the
configured Python;
@item the manual as @file{@var{infodir}/codebud.info}, registered with
@command{install-info}, so @kbd{info codebud} works.
@end itemize

@command{make uninstall} removes all of it again.

@subsection Configure options

@table @code
@item --prefix=@var{dir}
Install under @var{dir} instead of @file{/usr/local}, e.g.@:
@code{--prefix=$HOME/.local} for a per-user install without
@command{sudo}.
@item PYTHON=@var{path}
Use this Python (and its modules), e.g.@: @code{PYTHON=/usr/bin/python3.12}.
@item --disable-dependency-check
Do not require @code{requests} and @code{prompt_toolkit} at configure
time.  For distribution packages, which build in a clean chroot and declare
the dependencies themselves.
@end table

Run @command{./configure --help} for the standard options
(@option{--bindir}, @option{--infodir}, @dots{}).

@subsection Developer setup

To work on Codebud itself, let @command{bootstrap} also create a virtual
environment with the development tools (@pxref{Python Environment}) and
point @command{configure} at it:

@example
./bootstrap --dev
./configure PYTHON="$PWD/.venv/bin/python"
make && make check
@end example

@subsection Target reference

@multitable @columnfractions .28 .72
@headitem Target @tab Effect
@item @code{all} (default) @tab Build the launcher and the info manual
@item @code{check} @tab Run the test suite against the source tree
@item @code{install} / @code{uninstall} @tab Install / remove Codebud
@item @code{installcheck} @tab Check that the installed @command{codebud} runs and reports this version
@item @code{info}, @code{html}, @code{pdf} @tab Build the manual (@file{docs/})
@item @code{dist} @tab Build the release tarball
@item @code{distcheck} @tab Build the tarball and verify it builds, tests, installs and uninstalls cleanly from a read-only copy
@item @code{clean} @tab Remove what @command{make} built (keeps @file{codebud.info}, which ships in the tarball)
@item @code{distclean} @tab Also remove what @command{configure} generated
@item @code{maintainer-clean} @tab Back to a fresh checkout: also remove what @command{./bootstrap} generated, the release tarball and the developer caches (keeps @file{.venv/}); run @command{./bootstrap} again afterwards
@item @code{help} @tab List the targets
@item @code{install-dev} @tab Editable install with the @code{dev} group in @file{.venv/}
@item @code{lint}, @code{format} @tab @command{ruff check}; @command{ruff format} + @command{ruff check --fix}
@item @code{coverage} @tab Tests with branch coverage; report in @file{htmlcov/}
@item @code{dist-python} @tab Python sdist and wheel in @file{dist/}
@item @code{install-link} / @code{uninstall-link} @tab Link @file{.venv/bin/codebud} into @var{LINKDIR} (default @file{~/.local/bin}) / remove the link
@item @code{clean-dev} @tab Remove bytecode, tool caches and egg-info from the checkout (keeps @file{.venv/})
@item @code{clean-venv} @tab Remove @file{.venv/}
@end multitable

@var{LINKDIR} can be overridden: @code{make install-link LINKDIR=/usr/local/bin}.


@node Verifying the Install
@section Verifying the Install
@cindex verification

@subsection Run the test suite

@example
make check
@end example

All tests should pass.  No Ollama connection is needed for the tests.

@subsection Check the environment

Run the built-in health checker first.  It verifies Ollama is running,
the model is pulled, and every config file parses cleanly:

@example
codebud doctor
@end example

Each check prints @samp{[PASS]} or @samp{[FAIL]} with a one-line fix command.
Fix any failures before continuing.  A @samp{[WARN]} line (for example,
the embedding model @code{all-minilm} is not pulled) is optional:
it only matters for project search.

@subsection Smoke-test the agent

Pull the recommended model if you have not already (@pxref{Pulling a Model}),
then:

@example
export OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
codebud run "list files in the current directory"
@end example

You will see a live spinner while the model processes the prompt, then a
numbered plan summary, then the output of each step.  On a CPU-only laptop
the first request of a session takes about 30 seconds to plan with the 3B
model, and later requests about 8 seconds, because Ollama reuses the cached
start of the prompt; the spinner keeps moving the whole time so you know it
is not frozen (@pxref{Progress Output}).

@subsection Install the skill for other agents (optional)

Codebud ships skills that teach other agents, such as Claude Code or
OpenClaw, when and how to call it.  Install the default skill where your
agent looks for skills; print the Agensi marketplace variant with
@option{--for agensi}:

@example
codebud skill install --for claude     @r{# ~/.claude/skills/codebud/}
codebud skill install --for openclaw   @r{# ~/.openclaw/skills/codebud/}
codebud skill show --for agensi        @r{# Agensi hand-off skill}
@end example

@xref{The skill Subcommand}.

@subsection Troubleshooting

If @command{codebud doctor} reports failures or the agent returns a
@code{plan_error}, see @ref{Troubleshooting} for common causes and fixes.
